Expected Outcomes
A working Maven-based Camel application that periodically fetches posts and user data from a live public REST API
Transformed and enriched JSON output (uppercase titles, word counts, category tags) visible in console logs, along with a working health-check monitoring route
Verified error-handling behavior for both non-200 HTTP responses and 404 resource-not-found cases, confirmed independently with curl
Troubleshooting
Issue 1: Application exits immediately or mvn exec:java reports "No plugin found" Symptom: the build fails before any routes run. Diagnostic hint: run mvn clean compile first and check that the exec-maven-plugin version and mainClass in pom.xml match the class you intend to run; confirm with mvn -version that Java 11 is active.

Issue 2: No log output appears, or routes seem to hang Symptom: the console stays silent after "started successfully." Diagnostic hint: check outbound network connectivity with curl -I https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1, and verify simplelogger.properties is present under src/main/resources so SLF4J picks up your log level configuration.

Conclusion
In this lab, you built a complete Apache Camel application that consumes a live external REST API, transforms and enriches the JSON responses it receives, and stores the results through simulated downstream routes. You implemented comprehensive error handling for non-200 HTTP responses, rate-limiting scenarios, and general exceptions, ensuring the integration behaves predictably under real-world failure conditions. You also created a monitoring route that performs periodic health checks against the API, and you validated the entire pipeline using both the application's own logging and independent manual verification with curl.

Through this process you practiced several skills central to production integration work: configuring HTTP-based Camel routes with proper headers and methods, writing custom processors with Jackson to reshape and enrich JSON payloads, structuring multi-route Camel applications with direct endpoints for separation of concerns, and building layered error handling that distinguishes between API-level errors and transport-level exceptions. As next steps, consider persisting the transformed data to a real database instead of logging it, adding authenticated API calls using headers such as Authorization: Bearer <token>, introducing retry and circuit-breaker patterns for greater resilience, and packaging the application as a container image for deployment in a production-like environment. You are now well positioned to apply these techniques to real-world API integration projects.
